{{define "title"}}Driving this git server from an agent{{end}}
{{define "main"}}
<main class="doc stack">
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">repositories</a> / <span class="here">docs / agents</span></p>

  <div class="doc-body">
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <span class="label">On this page</span>
    <a href="#token">1 · Get a token</a>
    <a href="#base">2 · Address and credential</a>
    <a href="#read">3 · What an agent can read</a>
    <a href="#limits">4 · Rate limits</a>
    <a href="#errors">5 · Errors</a>
    <a href="#mcp">6 · A tool server for agents</a>
  </nav>
  <div class="stack">
    <h1 class="heading-page">Driving this git server from an agent</h1>
    <p class="lead">There is no SDK. An agent uses git and HTTP with a token you mint here. This page covers everything it needs.</p>

    <section id="token" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">1 · Get a token</h2>
      <p>Go to <a href="/tokens">agent tokens</a>, name the one repository the agent works in, choose <strong>read</strong> unless it must push, and pick the shortest lifetime that covers the job. The token is shown once and stored nowhere. Copy it straight away.</p>
      <p>Put it in the agent's environment as <code>ORIGO_TOKEN</code>. Do not put it in a repository or a prompt.</p>
      <p>Minting a token needs administrative access to the repository, and a person does it on this site. An agent cannot mint a token. There is no API for it.</p>
      <p>A token cannot be withdrawn. Minting writes nothing, so there is nothing to delete and no list to show. The lifetime is the only limit. The longest this server signs is {{.MaxTTL}}.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="base" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">2 · Address and credential</h2>
      <p>There are two addresses. <strong>These screens need a browser sign-in</strong> and do not accept a token. <strong>The git server accepts the token</strong>.</p>
      <pre class="scroller sample"># the git server
{{.APIBase}}

# clone, with the token as the password
git clone https://x-access-token:$ORIGO_TOKEN@{{.CloneHost}}/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.git

# read
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $ORIGO_TOKEN" \
  {{.RepoRead}}</pre>
      <p>Every API address starts with <code>/v1/repos/</code> and names a repository by its <strong>identifier</strong>. Owner and name do not work here. The identifier survives a rename. It appears in the addresses of these screens, and your administrator gives it to you. Owner and name work for git itself.</p>
      <p>Both credential forms are accepted: <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, and ordinary HTTP basic auth with the token as the password and any username, which is the form git itself sends.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="read" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">3 · What an agent can read</h2>
      <p>Reads are JSON, except three that are the bytes themselves: a comparison is a unified diff, a file is its own content, and an archive is a gzipped tar.</p>
      <div class="scroller">
        <table class="stackable">
          <caption>Every read an agent can make</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What you want</th>
              <th scope="col">Address, after <code>/v1/repos/&lt;id&gt;</code></th>
              <th scope="col">What comes back</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">the repository</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">(nothing)</td><td data-label="Answer">owner, name, default branch, head, size, when it was last pushed</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">branches and tags</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">/refs?prefix=refs/heads/</td><td data-label="Answer">name, object id, and a tag's target; at most 10,000</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">the commit log</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">/commits?ref=&amp;path=&amp;limit=&amp;cursor=</td><td data-label="Answer">newest first, 50 a page and at most 200; page on with the cursor</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">one commit</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">/commits/&lt;sha&gt;</td><td data-label="Answer">its message, parents, trailers, and file and line counts</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">a comparison</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">/compare/&lt;base&gt;...&lt;head&gt;?path=</td><td data-label="Answer">a unified diff, cut at 1 MiB on a file boundary</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">a directory</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">/tree/&lt;sha&gt;?path=&amp;cursor=</td><td data-label="Answer">one level, 5,000 entries a page</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">a file</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">/blob/&lt;sha&gt;</td><td data-label="Answer">the bytes; past 50 MiB you must ask for a range</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">a snapshot</th><td data-label="Address" class="path">/archive/&lt;sha&gt;.tar.gz</td><td data-label="Answer">the whole tree at that commit</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>A file and a directory are addressed by <strong>object id</strong>. Resolve a path through the directory listing first. Every answer names the commit it resolved to in <code>Origo-Commit</code>, so an agent quoting a file can give an exact reference instead of a branch name. An answer the server had to cut says so in <code>Origo-Truncated</code>, This is the only truncation an agent meets. The line budget that collapses a large file on these screens applies to the browser view only.</p>
      <p>Each read covers one repository. There is no repository listing, no file search and no blame.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">4 · Rate limits</h2>
      <p>One figure, in requests a minute, for each person or agent, counted per server. The default is 600, and an installation can set a different figure for one caller. There are no tiers. A token is held to the figure of whoever minted it.</p>
      <p>Every answer carries the figure in force, so an agent can pace itself:</p>
      <pre class="scroller sample">RateLimit-Limit: 600</pre>
      <p>Past it the answer is <code>429</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> in whole seconds. Retrying sooner is refused again. Wait exactly that long. Do not add backoff.</p>
      <p>Under load the server also answers <code>429</code> when it is running as much git work as it allows at once. A request waits a few seconds for a slot first. Retry that one too.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="errors" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">5 · Errors</h2>
      <p>Every refusal is the same object. Branch on <code>code</code>, which is stable. <code>message</code> is one sentence for a person. <code>details</code> varies by code and names the reference, the limit or the reason.</p>
      <pre class="scroller sample">{
  "error": {
    "code": "forbidden",
    "message": "You do not have permission to do this.",
    "details": { "reason": "token scope does not allow this action" }
  }
}</pre>
      <div class="scroller">
        <table class="stackable">
          <caption>The refusals an agent meets, and what to do about each</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Status</th>
              <th scope="col">code</th>
              <th scope="col">Does waiting help?</th>
              <th scope="col">What the agent should do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">400</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">invalid_request</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">The request is wrong. Fix it or stop; do not repeat it.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">401</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">unauthenticated</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">The token is missing, malformed, or past its expiry. Stop and tell the person: only a person can mint another.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">403</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">forbidden</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">Stop. Report the reason in <code>details</code>; a scope too narrow and another repository both land here.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">404</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">repo_not_found</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">The repository is not there, or you may not see it. The server will not say which.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">404</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">ref_not_found</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">The reference or object id is wrong, or it has been collected.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">409</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">non_fast_forward</td><td data-label="Waiting">once</td><td data-label="Do">Fetch, rebase, push once more. Then stop.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">413</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">over_quota</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">The repository is at its storage limit. Stop and tell the person.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">413</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">blob_too_large</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">Ask for the file in ranges of at most 50 MiB.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">429</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">rate_limited</td><td data-label="Waiting">yes</td><td data-label="Do">Sleep exactly <code>Retry-After</code>. No backoff needed.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">503</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">storage_unavailable</td><td data-label="Waiting">yes</td><td data-label="Do">Nothing was lost. Retry after the header, or in a few minutes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">503</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">authorizer_unavailable</td><td data-label="Waiting">yes</td><td data-label="Do">Permissions cannot be checked right now. Retry in a few minutes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">504</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">operation_timeout</td><td data-label="Waiting">no</td><td data-label="Do">The operation took too long and changed nothing. Ask for less.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Most of these are terminal. Retrying an expired token spends the rate budget its replacement needs. No field says whether waiting helps. Use the table above. The refusals worth waiting on carry <code>Retry-After</code>.</p>
      <p>Every answer, refusal or not, carries <code>Origo-Contract</code>. Its value is the version of everything on this page. It changes when something here stops being true.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="mcp" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">6 · A tool server for agents</h2>
      <p><strong>This is planned. It is not built.</strong> There is nothing to install and nothing to configure yet. Everything above this section works today.</p>
      <p>The plan is a small separate program you run beside an agent. It speaks the Model Context Protocol over standard input and output, and turns the reads above into tools an agent calls by name. It would take a token of the kind you mint on this site, hold to the same rate limit, and return the same refusals. It would do nothing a plain HTTP agent cannot.</p>
      <p>It will have no tool for minting a token. A person creates every credential.</p>
      <p>Until it exists, an agent uses git and the reads in section 3.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  </div>
</main>
{{end}}
